# Two Pints - a milk round, a doorstep that is still being paid for, and what a person does about it.
# Written for the library. One person, one small order, and the question of how far a job reaches.

title: Two Pints
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
Three hundred and forty bottles on a Tuesday and you know all of them by the doorstep they
stand on: the two pints at the end of Chandlers Lane, the six at the shop, the gold top at
number 9 which has been gold top since 1994 and nobody has ever explained it.
You start at half past four and you are done by eight, and you have been doing it for
twenty-one years, and the round is a thing you carry in your head rather than on a sheet.
-> the_order

== the_order ==
The two pints at the end of Chandlers Lane have been on the book since before you had the
round. They are paid by direct debit and the address on the book is Mr A. Ellery and the
bottles come back empty on the Saturday and always have.
Three weeks ago a woman you have not seen before left a note in the empty bottle.
-> the_note

== the_note ==
It was on the back of a shopping list and it said *please keep leaving them* and it had a
phone number on it and nothing else, and you have been leaving them, and now it is the
Thursday of the fourth week and the bottles are not coming back empty.
They are coming back full, which is a thing that happens at a house where somebody is
not drinking the milk and nobody has told the dairy, and you have delivered eleven pints to
a doorstep where the milk is standing outside all day and then coming in at night by
somebody who is not drinking it.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
The dairy is a phone call and a form and the order comes off the book. That is the whole of
what the job asks of you: you deliver what is on the book and you ring the office if
something is wrong with the book.
You have been doing it for twenty-one years and you have made that call four times, and one
of them was a man in a hospice who died on the Sunday and the family wrote to the depot to
say thank you for stopping the milk.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
You are at the end of the lane with an empty crate and eleven bottles standing on a step in
the cold, and the woman's phone number is in your pocket where it has been for three weeks.
* Ring the office and take it off the book. It is what the job asks. -> ring_office
* Knock. Somebody is in there taking the milk in at night. -> knock
* Ring her. She left a number, and that is what a number is for. -> ring_her
* Keep leaving them, and say nothing to anybody. -> keep_leaving

== ring_office ==
You ring the office at half past eight and give them the address and they take it off the
book, and the last delivery is the Friday, and there is no note on the doorstep on the
Saturday because there is no bottle to leave one in.
The dairy does it properly: somebody rings the number on the account and something is
arranged, and you are not told what, and you do not ask, because that is the office and this
is the lane.
-> ending_office

== knock ==
You knock at half past six with a crate under your arm, which is early, and you have
half-prepared something to say about the milk, and the door is opened by a man of about
forty in a dressing gown who has clearly not slept.
man: You're the milk.
you: I'm the milk.
man: She's in the home at Wren. She's been in the home since the second week. I'm here doing
the house up and I didn't want to stop the milk because it's the last thing that's still
coming here.
-> the_note_man

== the_note_man ==
You stand on the step with the crate and he stands in the doorway and neither of you says
anything for a moment, and then he says the thing that is the whole of it, which is that he
is drinking two pints of milk a day and hates milk, and has been doing it since the second
week because the alternative is a doorstep with nothing on it.
He asks you to keep leaving them. He says it in the way people say things they have thought
about for three weeks.
-> ending_the_step

== ring_her ==
You ring the number at ten past seven in the evening, which is a reasonable hour, and she
answers on the second ring like somebody who has been waiting for a phone call and does not
want to say so.
She is the daughter. Her mother went into Wren in the second week of the month and the milk
is still going and she knows it is still going, and she has been leaving it because
stopping it felt like a thing you do when somebody dies or when they are not coming back.
-> the_call

== the_call ==
She asks you what the milk is doing. You tell her the truth, which is that it is standing on
the step all day and going in at night, and that she is not drinking it, and there is a
pause in which the two of you both know exactly who is.
Then she laughs, which you had not expected, and it is a real one, and she says of course
he is, and then she says the other thing, which is: do not stop it.
-> ending_left_on


== keep_leaving ==
You keep leaving them. There is no decision in it and no note and no phone call, and the
eleven bottles become sixteen and then become a crate the dairy has to send somebody out
about, and that is the December, and you are still leaving two pints at the end of
Chandlers Lane in the January when the order comes off the book by itself because the
direct debit has stopped.
-> END

== ending_office ==
The order comes off the book and nobody rings the lane about it and the doorstep is empty on
the Saturday and stays empty.
You go past it twice a day for months and it is a step like any other step now, and in the
March there is a van outside and a man carrying boxes, and in the June there is a young
couple, and there is no reason for either of you to know the other exists. That is the job:
you deliver what is on the book and you ring the office when something is wrong with the
book. It is a good job and it does not reach as far as a doorstep.
-> END

== ending_the_step ==
You keep leaving them, and you never mention it to the office, and he drinks two pints of
milk a day and hates milk.
It goes on until the March, which is when the house is sold, and on the last Friday there is
a note in the bottle in handwriting you have now seen three times, and it says *thank you
for not stopping it* and it has no number on it, and there is nothing to do with it, so you
put it in the cab of the van and it is in there still.
-> END

== ending_left_on ==
You do not stop it, and the dairy does not ring anybody, and the milk goes on standing on
the step all day and going in at night.
In the April there is a note in the bottle that says *she is home on the 12th* and on the
13th both bottles come back empty, which is the only piece of news you get about any of it,
and which is exactly enough, and the first time you see her at the door of the lane in the
summer you say good morning the way you say it to three hundred and forty other doorsteps
and she says good morning the way people do when they have never had a conversation with
somebody, and neither of you says one word more than that.
-> END
